      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To track down the actual memory usage, you need deep introspection tools beyond standard Kubernetes events. Use the `kubectl top pod <pod-name>` command to get basic CPU/Memory usage snapshots, but for process-level detail, you'll need to execute commands inside the container if possible via `kubectl exec`. Consider using an application performance monitoring (APM) tool or running tools like `ps` or `top` inside the running container to identify the specific process consuming the memory. This move from Kubernetes limits to application profiling is where the leak hunt truly begins.",
